{
  "evidence": "TEXT_E: Five early pledges to cut infant class sizes and NHS waiting lists by 100,000; a welfare‑to‑work plan for 250,000 under‑25s; no rise in income tax rates; and a clean‑up agenda (Freedom of Information, party‑funding transparency). Strong focus on schools (smaller classes, literacy/numeracy targets) and NHS (end internal market, shift money to frontline care). TEXT_F: Emphasis on low taxes (target 20p basic rate), EU sovereignty and resisting the Social Chapter; NHS real‑terms rises and GP fundholding; education testing and more grammar/specialist schools; tough law‑and‑order measures.",
  "reasoning": "In 1997, voters’ top concerns were the NHS, education, jobs/unemployment, crime, and political ‘sleaze’; TEXT_E makes concrete, measurable front‑page pledges on NHS waiting lists and class sizes, a jobs programme, and cleaning up politics. TEXT_F addresses health, education, and crime but foregrounds tax cuts, privatization, and EU sovereignty, which were not the dominant public priorities, so TEXT_E better matches what voters cared about.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "TEXT_E"
}
